n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{5}{2}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084598/state.json" "file_sha256" "969f498a9a2cb95488c3c476fc809e0e27414b43e1064e144d417e68a2506a0e"} {"question" "q-275128" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6553ce49f1dd32c15067d684ebadb16ba4ee0ca4cfa3e561243a07f5a0662d7b"} {"question" "q-275128" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084598/state.json" "file_sha256" "969f498a9a2cb95488c3c476fc809e0e27414b43e1064e144d417e68a2506a0e"} {"question" "q-275128" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084598/state.json" "file_sha256" "969f498a9a2cb95488c3c476fc809e0e27414b43e1064e144d417e68a2506a0e"} {"question" "q-275128" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084598/state.json" "file_sha256" "969f498a9a2cb95488c3c476fc809e0e27414b43e1064e144d417e68a2506a0e"} {"question" "q-275128" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084598/state.json" "file_sha256" "969f498a9a2cb95488c3c476fc809e0e27414b43e1064e144d417e68a2506a0e"} {"question" "q-278290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084598/assets/36aeda6b6f5ce46552f55f458da60ef46a646d737c452d586389c03c47d1c9cb.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=f,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OG}=g$ and $P$ divides $\\overset{―}{FG}$ in the ratio $FP:PG=8:5.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OP}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084598/state.json" "file_sha256" "969f498a9a2cb95488c3c476fc809e0e27414b43e1064e144d417e68a2506a0e"} {"question" "q-278290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FP}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=f,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{FP}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OFG,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FG} & =\\overset{\\to }{FO}+\\overset{\\to }{OG} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{OG} \\\\  & =-f+g \\\\  & =g-f.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $FP:PG=8:5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{FP}=\\frac{8}{13}\\overset{\\to }{FG}=\\frac{8}{13}(g-f).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OP} & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FP} \\\\  & =f+\\frac{8}{13}(g-f) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}f+\\frac{8}{13}g.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084598/state.json" "file_sha256" "969f498a9a2cb95488c3c476fc809e0e27414b43e1064e144d417e68a2506a0e"} {"question" "q-278290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084598/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6553ce49f1dd32c15067d684ebadb16ba4ee0ca4cfa3e561243a07f5a0662d7b"} {"questi